{:fh/id  "FH-CTRL-008"
 :fh/law "Work from a superseded generation does not land in either direction: a commit whose generation no longer matches the committed record produces no caller intent and moves nothing, an edit made under a generation the caller has already replaced is born stale and is neither displayed nor committable, a cancel clears the record before a later blur can resurrect it, and a repeated commit is an idempotent no-op — every one of them decided in the handler against committed state, never by a guard captured during render."

 :kind :my.ui/buffered-field

 :control [:invoice 42 :amount]

 ;; The caller's baseline, the generation that established it, and the
 ;; generation the caller advances to when it replaces that baseline.
 :baseline      "10.00"
 :revision      4
 :next-revision 5

 ;; Where an accepted draft goes — the caller's own domain event, at the
 ;; caller's own path.
 :on-commit      [:invoice/amount-committed 42]
 :committed-path [:invoice 42 :amount]

 ;; --- The current commit: the control, so nothing below is green for
 ;; --- want of a working commit at all.
 :ordinary
 {:typed            "12."
  :committed-value  "12."
  :record-after     nil}

 ;; --- A commit whose generation the caller has replaced.
 ;;
 ;; The intent an event site fires always carries the LAST COMMITTED
 ;; render's generation — a candidate the host never selected published
 ;; no event bodies at all — so once the reset render commits, the blur
 ;; the node carries speaks for generation 5 while the record still
 ;; speaks for 4. It is a no-op: no caller intent, no record change.
 :superseded-commit
 {:typed                  "12."
  :committed-value        nil
  :record-after           {:reset-key 4 :draft "12."}
  :displayed-after        "10.00"}

 ;; --- An edit made under a generation the caller has already replaced.
 ;;
 ;; The edit is materialized from the render at generation 4 and lands
 ;; after the caller's reset to 5 has been processed. It stamps the
 ;; record with the generation its render displayed, so the record is
 ;; BORN STALE: never shown, and not committable at 5 either. The reset
 ;; wins — which is the safe direction, because a keystroke aimed at a
 ;; baseline the caller has withdrawn must not reinstate it.
 :superseded-edit
 {:typed             "!!"
  :record-after      {:reset-key 4 :draft "!!"}
  :displayed-after   "10.00"
  :committed-value   nil}

 ;; --- Cancel, and the blur that was already on its way.
 ;;
 ;; Cancel clears the record. The blur behind it consults committed
 ;; state, finds no live edit, and produces nothing — Escape beats a late
 ;; blur rather than racing it.
 :cancel
 {:typed            "12."
  :record-after     nil
  :committed-value  nil
  :displayed-after  "10.00"}

 ;; --- Repeated commits.
 ;;
 ;; The first commit retires the session; the second finds no record and
 ;; is a no-op, so a double blur does not dispatch the caller's event
 ;; twice.
 :repeated
 {:typed             "12."
  :commits           2
  :committed-value   "12."
  :caller-events     1}}
